    A bulk JPQL update is executed directly in the database and bypasses the persistence context.
    Any Product entity already loaded in the current persistence context (the first-level cache)
    keeps its old price, so a later find or query in the same transaction would return stale data.
    Worse, if such a stale entity is also modified, Hibernate would flush it and overwrite the
    discounted price. We therefore call flush() before the update (so pending changes are not lost)
    and clear() after it, which detaches all entities and forces them to be reloaded from the database.
    Note also that bulk updates do not increment @Version
























